WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.780
Space, the final frontier.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:15.740
Except, we don't have cool spaceships because the US government has a bit of a thing for aircraft carriers,  F35's, and ships bristling with guns.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.420
The United States spends roughly 600 billion dollars per year on the military.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.300
Which is about 54% of the annual discretionary budget.

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:31.460
By comparison, NASA only gets 18.5 billion dollars per year, or less than 0.5% of the national budget.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.160
Now, we are not going to get tied up in debating whether or not the US needs such a massive military.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.200
But, here are some quick facts in case you're interested.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.040
The next biggest spender is China, at a 190 billion dollars per year.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:47.200
China has 500 of their type-99 tanks, which are outclassed by the M1 abrams.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.060
Of which, the US has 8700.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.680
The US has 10 aircraft carriers.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.640
The rest of the world combined has 10 smaller ones.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.680
There are 8,400 attack helicopters around the world.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.440
Of these, the united states owns 6,400.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:01.660
And those are just the vehicles.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.840
One single tomahawk missile costs around 1.5 million dollars.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.320
Then you have paychecks, fuel, ammunition, and all the rest.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:13.440
All those expenses add up to that whopping figure of 600 Billion dollars per year!

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.700
The general public's interest in space exploration has gradually dwindled since the space race.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.340
And now that there are no pesky communists trying to steal the moon from it's rightful owners...

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.480
We haven't felt the need to spend significant resources on new missions!

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.720
But what if, by some strange events, NASA ended up with the military's budget?

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:29.740
What could they do with it?

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:30.960
For a little perspective...

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.880
The entire cost of the Apollo Program was $136 billion in 2007 dollars.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.360
$136 billion over 13 years,

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.740
Or just over $10billion dollars per year.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.320
Picture a clock representing $600 billion dollars.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.500
One minute on that clock is $10 billion dollars.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.120
One of the greatest scientific achievements in history fits into that tiny sliver of the US military budget.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.420
Pretty crazy.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:58.420
We won't fuss about the little things like bureaucracy and research and development timelines in this video,

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.060
we'll just think about the cool stuff.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.780
First, NASA loves telescopes, probes and satellites.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:04.940
With a massive budget,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.140
the 10 or 20 years it would've taken to launch all of their projects

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.140
could be cut down to just one or two years.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.980
We would see telescopes that'll make Hubble seem like a Happy Meal toy.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:17.300
Infrastructure upgrades could be implemented to allow faster transmission of data from new missions.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.260
With all those resources in orbit,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.700
there would be new discoveries much more frequently,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.400
both on earth and in the cosmos.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.980
That could rekindle the public's thirst for exploration.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:31.200
Possibly the smartest decision NASA could make would be to fund some smaller privately owned space exploration groups,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.540
like SpaceX.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:34.540
Which has an excellent track record of pushing the envelope.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.840
They could fund new space stations, reuseable space planes, asteroid mining operation and lunar bases

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.200
Pushing further out into science fiction territory,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:47.960
once a lunar base is established, electromagnetic launchers on the moon could put large ships or space stations in lunar orbit,

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.960
or a trajectory to Mars and beyond.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.740
That would definitely be the fastest way to colonize Mars,

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:55.720
having a sort of halfway house on the moon to stage future missions.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:00.520
The International Space Station costed about $100 billion spread out over a decade,

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.580
so think of the enormous, highly advanced stations that could be built with six times that amount per year!

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:11.040
The kind of money and a massive base of operations in orbit could support a core of thousands of active astronauts,

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.320
With a few hundred at a time exploring the solar system in different simultaneous missions.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:18.880
Serious planning could begin for manned mission into the Jovian System,

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.300
Though, it might take several decades to become feasible, even with the massive budget.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.100
Then there's this beast with NASA had been putting off for years.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.000
A Rotating Wheel Space Station.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.560
This design has been a staple in science fiction and popular speculation since the early 20th century.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.600
The rotation would provide artificial gravity, something prevalent in films like 2001 Space odyssey

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.600
but not yet achieved in reality.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:43.500
A giant rotating wheel space station would could be a mobile base of operations near any planet we wanted to colonize

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.000
and can be used as a safety net if something went wrong and the colony had to be temporarily evacuated.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.060
Or, if multiple stations were built,

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.460
They could be place strategically around the solar system as rest stops for journeys into deep space.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.620
Speaking of colonizing planets,

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:02.500
with a 600 billion dollar annual budget it would be feasible to colonize Mars in as little as 10 years.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:06.560
If the entire budget were spent on transporting people and supplies to Mars on shared rockets,

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.260
in 10 years we can potentially see a populations of 40,000 people.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:10.880
Or,

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.840
If NASA wanted to test the waters before sending a large population,

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.820
they could spend the entire budget to send about 14 people per year, in their own individual rockets

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.440
and provide each person with over an acre of Martian terrain to call their own.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.400
Then there the intriguing celestial bodies, currently out of reach to mankind.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:28.760
We could send robotic explorers to them all.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.540
Aircraft to Venus and Titan.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.920
Submarines to Europa.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.060
Imagine what discoveries could be made in the vast ocean beneath Europa's icy surface.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.620
We could be just a few years away from uncovering extra-terrestrial life,

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:41.920
if only NASA had the military's pocket book.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:43.820
Of course, these are all hypothetical's,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.700
but with that kind of funding to play with, it's obvious that the scientific world would be absolutely inundated with mountains of new data

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.800
and off world colonies could become a reality, easily within our lifetime

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.460
and maybe even interstellar travel.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.880
Who knows, maybe some Russian hackers will decide that it's time for another space race

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:00.100
and switch around some zeros in the national budget.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:01.680
A man can only dream.

